100_{64} = 1 \cdot 64^2 + 0 \cdot 64^1 + 0 \cdot 64^0 = 1 \cdot 4096 + 0 \cdot 64 + 0 \cdot 1 = 4096_{10}
\] Next, we need to convert the base-10 number \(4096_{10}\) to its base-62 equivalent. To do this, we repeatedly divide the number by 62 and keep track of the remainders. Let's do this step-by-step: \[
\begin{aligned}
4096 \div 62 &= 66 \text{ with remainder } 4 \\
66 \div 62 &= 1 \text{ with remainder } 4 \\
1 \div 62 &= 0 \text{ with remainder } 1 \\
\end{aligned}
